tmpclaude-*
*.log
.DS_Store
prd.json

# Screenshots from browser testing
.claude/screenshots/

# Backup and archive files
prd-backup-*.json
prd-archive-*.json

# Session handoffs
handoff-*.md

# Local settings overrides
.claude/settings.local.json

# Agent worktrees. Nested git dirs, created per-agent by workflow isolation and by
# `git worktree add`. Ignored narrowly rather than ignoring .claude/ wholesale,
# because the two entries above show the partial tracking here is deliberate.
.claude/worktrees/

# Session analytics
ledger.json

# Auto mode flag (session-specific)
.claude/auto-active

# Session carriers and verbatim user prompts (autodev-memory). The directory
# also writes its own .gitignore, so this line is belt-and-braces.
.claude/memory-sessions/

# Leftover scratch that was tracked before these rules existed
.claude/commit-msg.txt

# Knowledge auto-injection throttle state. Rewritten on each update to hold only
# the current session's "sessionId<TAB>area" markers, so it stays bounded to the
# active session's areas rather than accumulating across sessions.
.claude/knowledge-surfaced

# Update backups and compact state
settings.backup.json
.claude/pre-compact-state.json

# Node dependencies
node_modules/

# Environment variables
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Build artifacts
dist/
build/
.next/

# find-vacuous-assertions.js crash-recovery backups
*.vacuity-backup

# Generated reports. rules/file-organization.md gives these 7-day retention and
# they are never tracked. (Other paths under .claude/ ARE tracked, deliberately --
# see "partial tracking under .claude/ is deliberate" below. This parenthetical
# used to read "0 files under .claude/ are" and was false: `git ls-files .claude/`
# answers it, and a count in a comment is right only on the day it is typed.)
# They are ignored because
# this repo is PUBLIC and a generated report quotes whatever it swept -- one here
# named a CLIENT project 8 times, which check-no-private-names caught in the
# working tree. Ignoring the directory closes the exposure rather than hiding it:
# the checker uses --exclude-standard, so an ignored file cannot be staged at all.
.claude/reports/

# Three cases the line above does NOT cover, each found by pointing
# check-no-private-names.js at the working tree on 2026-08-26 after teaching it
# to flag absolute home paths. All three were untracked-but-not-ignored, and
# .claude/ is deliberately not ignored wholesale here, so every one of them was
# a single `git add -A` away from a PUBLIC repo.
#
# 1. The DOUBLED path. `.claude/.claude/reports/` is a real directory on disk,
#    produced by something resolving a relative `.claude` against a cwd that was
#    already inside one. `.claude/reports/` does not match it. Worth fixing at
#    the writer too; ignoring it here only stops the leak.
.claude/.claude/

# 2. Session resume scratch. `.claude/RESUME.md` was already ignored, but files
#    like `.claude/resume-<topic>.md` were not, and they carry the same content.
.claude/resume-*.md
.claude/memory-session-id

# 3. Per-agent memory. Persistent locally and still machine-local state: the
#    reviewer's own notes carried a full home path.
.claude/agent-memory/

# Session handoff. Names the repos a session was working across, which in this
# estate includes private and client codebases. Never tracked; ignored so it
# cannot be staged into a PUBLIC repo by a careless `git add -A`.
.claude/RESUME.md

# preview_start config. Its entries carry absolute paths to whichever repos this
# machine serves, so it names private projects by construction.
.claude/launch.json

# Design canvases and preview scratch. Product work from OTHER repos lands here
# while a session iterates on it; this repo is public, so an accidental
# `git add -A` would publish it. Named narrowly, in the style above, because the
# partial tracking under .claude/ is deliberate.
.claude/design/
.claude/preview/

# 4. Panel deny state, written per-worktree by brain-panels.js. It lists the
#    sessions and repo paths a coordinator is silencing, so it names private and
#    client repos by construction. Found the same way as the three above: it
#    appeared in a worktree mid-session on 2026-08-29 and turned `npm test` red
#    for the session working there, because validate runs check-no-private-names
#    over untracked-but-unignored files and pre-push runs validate. A tool that
#    writes machine-local state into a PUBLIC repo must not be able to stage it.
.claude/panel-deny.json

# 5. Archives, including worktree quick-resume patches. file-organization gives
#    `.claude/archives/` 30-day retention for prd archives and backups, so a
#    file sitting there is doing its job -- but the directory was never ignored,
#    and a `.patch` written there is a full diff of whatever branch produced it,
#    in a PUBLIC repo. Found 2026-09-02 as a three-day-old stray that made
#    `npm run gate` exit 2 before check:suites ran a single suite: that script
#    rewrites sources in place, so it refuses any dirty tree, and one untracked
#    file nobody owned blocked verification for every session in this clone.
.claude/archives/

# Function-hooks API declarations, written per install by tooling/extract-plugin-types.js
# (or /plugin-types in a session). Vendor text, marked early access; never committed.
.claude/types/

# 6. Ad-hoc screenshot and handoff directories. Both were untracked-but-unignored
#    on 2026-09-18, found as a `+91 -0` Create-PR prompt in the desktop UI rather
#    than by a checker: `check-no-private-names.js` read them and stayed clean,
#    because neither happened to carry a name or an absolute path THAT DAY. The
#    exposure is prospective, which is why the rule is the directory and not the
#    contents. `.claude/screenshots/` has been ignored since line 7 and
#    `eb11-shots` is the same thing under an ad-hoc name, so the gap is that this
#    list is per-path: a session inventing a new directory gets no protection
#    from the rule covering its twin. 7.0 MB of product PNGs and a session
#    handoff that quotes whatever the session was doing, in a PUBLIC repo.
#    file-organization.md already gives handoffs 7-day retention, so both were
#    meant to be transient and nothing enforced it.
.claude/eb11-shots/
.claude/handoffs/

# 7. The same per-path gap on 2026-09-25: `.claude/deliveries/` (files staged
#    for a user) and `.claude/shots/` (another screenshot twin) showed up
#    untracked in a session's status. Same exposure, same fix.
.claude/deliveries/
.claude/shots/
